rent and grandchild conversing on an hourglass\">\r\n</td></tr>\r\n<tr><td height=\"10\" style=\"height: 10px; font-size: 0;\"></td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 0 3.5%; line-height: 1.3;\"><span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size: 13px; color: #848484;\">\r\n<p>Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images</p>\r\n</span></td></tr>\r\n<tr><td height=\"15\" style=\"height: 15px; font-size: 0;\">&nbsp;</td></tr>\r\n<tr><td class=\"v\" style=\"padding: 0 3.5%; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 17px; font-family: Georgia, serif; line-height: 1.75; color: #222222; letter-spacing: -0.1px;\">\r\n<p><strong>The gift of grandparenthood</strong> offers the chance to help raise a child, pass on traditions and get more fit chasing a toddler around.</p><ul><li>👵 <strong>Research suggests</strong> the role may also be linked to healthier aging and, in some cases, a longer life.</li></ul><p>⏳ <strong>The big picture: </strong>Grandparents who hit the sweet spot of involvement — not overextending, but being present and supportive — can experience physical, cognitive and emotional benefits, <em>Axios' Natalie Daher writes.</em></p><p><strong>Geriatric psychiatrist</strong> Marc Agronin recently outlined some of those benefits in The Wall Street Journal (<em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47043846.\" target=\"_blank\">gift link</a></em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47043846.\" target=\"_blank\"></a>):</p><p>1. 💪 <strong>It requires a can-do spirit:</strong> Children come with activities, schedules and the need for an adult in the room as they learn how to navigate the world, including sibling squabbles.   </p><ul><li><strong>Stepping up </strong>for these tasks can help build a grandparent's sense of competence and usefulness, and in turn a more positive view of aging.</li><li><strong>Yale psychologist</strong> Becca Levy has found that people with positive beliefs about aging lived, on average, <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47043846.430637/aHR0cHM6Ly9wdWJtZWQubmNiaS5ubG0ubmloLmdvdi8xMjE1MDIyNi8_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB101e98df\" target=\"_blank\">7½ years</a> longer.</li></ul><p>2. ❤️ <strong>It</strong> <strong>provides purpose:</strong> Grandparenting offers another vantage point on parenting — and, sometimes, a chance to do things differently from the first go-round.</p><ul><li><strong>Cooking family recipes</strong> with a new, younger partner in crime, for example, offers an opportunity to pass on wisdom and history.</li></ul><p>3. 👟 <strong>You gotta keep up: </strong>Grandparenting can lead to plenty of physical activity when you're trying to match the energy of a kid. </p><ul><li><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47043846.430637/aHR0cHM6Ly9wdWJtZWQubmNiaS5ubG0ubmloLmdvdi8zMzI5MTk0NS8_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBedf074d7\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>One study</strong></a> Agronin highlights found that moderate involvement in caring for grandchildren was associated with fewer mobility limitations, fewer depressive symptoms and better cognition, compared with no involvement.</li></ul><p>💞 <strong>The bottom line: </strong>The bond between grandparents and grandchildren is special and lasting — and benefits three generations.</p>\r\n</td></tr>\r\n<tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47043846.430637/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtMjY4MjhjNjQtZTdmZS00ZWJkLWJhOWUtMjJiNTUzOGFhOTVhLmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QxJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJTIzc3RvcnkxJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB6de6b1a4\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><